      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3 x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3 z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{3 x z}{y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t^{2},y=t,$ and $z=t^{3}$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3 t^{3} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3 t^{4})\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3 t^{2} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{3})\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(1)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((3 t^{3} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3 t^{4})\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3 t^{2} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((2 t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(1)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(3 t^{3} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (2 t)+(3 t^{4})\\cdot (1)+(3 t^{2} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =(6 t^{4} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t^{4})+(9 t^{4} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =3 t^{4} \\left(5 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
